   "note": "Added from the FAIRsharing Toxicology collection on 2026-10-05. Research 5 Oct 2026 (medium confidence): Added from the FAIRsharing Toxicology collection on 2026-10-05. FAIRsharing year 2013 replaced by 2008: NIH papers say the DSLD was launched in 2008 (J Food Compos Anal 2021, doi 10.1016/j.jfca.2021.104058) and created in 2008 (J Nutr 2018, doi 10.1093/jn/nxy082). 2013 matches the move to dsld.nlm.nih.gov (earliest archive capture 2013-05-09; FASEB J 2013 abstract doi 10.1096/fasebj.27.1_supplement.848.1). A predecessor, NLM's Dietary Supplements Labels Database, was online by Oct 2007 (archive). The homepage returns 403 to scripted requests but is live (archived 2026-08-01). Scope Life Sciences because it is a nutrition and public-health resource; toxicology uses it for supplement exposure and harmful ingredients. Verified 5 Oct 2026: datePublished changed (The row's own dateSource (Saldanha et al., JFCA 2021, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfca.2021.104058; full text PMC8336909) says a DSLD prototype was discussed at a project meeting in late 2008 and demonstrated in 2009, and 'The first version was deployed in 2013' with about 17,000 labels. The abstract's 'Launched in 2008' therefore refers to the project, not public availability. 2013 is supported by the April 2013 FASEB J abstract 'DSLD Will Capture Information from Dietary Supplement (DS) Labels' (doi 10.1096/fasebj.27.1_supplement.848.1), the earliest dsld.nlm.nih.gov capture (2013-05-09) and FAIRsharing year 2013. The basis stays release.) Proposed 9 Oct 2026: A database that indexes and serves records under stable identifiers, typed Domain-specific registry as databases are in FIPs.",
