   "creator": "IUPAC / NIST / InChI Trust",
   "url": "https://www.inchi-trust.org",
   "description": "The IUPAC International Chemical Identifier (InChI) is an open algorithm and text string that encodes a chemical structure in a canonical, layered form; the hashed InChIKey is a fixed-length version for searching. It gives chemical substances a unique, machine-readable identifier, so chemical records can be linked and deduplicated across databases.",
   "maintained": "active",
   "manuscript": "named",
   "manuscriptEvidence": "I Introduction; L7",
   "mentionedIn": [
    "Whitepaper",
    "INTOXICOM 1"
   ],
   "fairsharingRecord": "FAIRsharing.ddk9t9",
   "fairsharingMatch": "confirmed",
   "note": "Research 5 Oct 2026 (high confidence): The IUPAC project 2000-025-1-800 page (last updated 14 Apr 2005) says 'April 2005: Version 1 of InChI has now been released'. The SourceForge project was created 2005-04-16, and Heller et al. 2013 (doi:10.1186/1758-2946-5-7) give 'public introduction in 2005'. Earlier test versions (2002-2004, available on request) and a 2003 ICIC Nîmes conference paper came before the standard. I used the v1 release. FAIRsharing.ddk9t9 (Standard/identifier_schema, year 2005) was found through DataCite/Bioregistry and confirmed with one lookup. The latest software is v1.07 (2024). Verified 5 Oct 2026: fairsharingMatch changed (One JSON lookup of https://fairsharing.org/FAIRsharing.ddk9t9 returns 'IUPAC International Chemical Identifier' (InChI), Standard/identifier_schema, status ready, homepage https://www.inchi-trust.org/. Name and homepage match.) Proposed 9 Oct 2026: Declared in a FIP for F1, it gives each chemical structure a unique, canonical identifier and a fixed-length hashed InChIKey.",
