   "fsrType": [
    "https://w3id.org/fair/fip/terms/Knowledge-representation-language"
   ],
   "scope": "Life Sciences",
   "subdomains": [
    "computational and predictive toxicology"
   ],
   "datePublished": "1987-08",
   "dateSource": "https://nepis.epa.gov/Exe/ZyPDF.cgi/2000CAUR.PDF?Dockey=2000CAUR.PDF",
   "creator": "David Weininger (US EPA Duluth) and Daylight Chemical Information Systems; OpenSMILES by the Blue Obelisk community",
   "url": "http://opensmiles.org/opensmiles.html",
   "description": "Line notation that writes a chemical structure as a short ASCII string, with canonical and isomeric forms and an open community specification (OpenSMILES). It is used to exchange, store and search structures and as input to QSAR, read-across and other in silico toxicology tools.",
   "maintained": "active",
   "manuscript": "absent",
   "mentionedIn": [
    "FAIRSharing"
   ],
   "fairsharingRecord": "FAIRsharing.qv4b3c",
   "fairsharingMatch": "confirmed",
   "note": "Added from the FAIRsharing Toxicology collection on 2026-10-05. Research 5 Oct 2026 (high confidence): Added from the FAIRsharing Toxicology collection on 2026-10-05. FAIRsharing year 1980 replaced: SMILES was first formally described by Weininger in J Chem Inf Comput Sci, Feb 1988 (doi 10.1021/ci00057a005). It was developed in the 1980s at the US EPA Duluth laboratory and later maintained by Daylight. The FAIRsharing record and homepage describe OpenSMILES, the open community specification, which was online by 2007-11-01 (archive). The https version of opensmiles.org does not respond, so the url stays on http. Verified 5 Oct 2026: datePublished changed (SMILES was first formally described in a US EPA Environmental Research Brief, EPA/600/M-87/021: Anderson, Veith and Weininger, 'SMILES: A Line Notation and Computerized Interpreter for Chemical Structures', ERL-Duluth, dated 'August 1987' on its cover (https://nepis.epa.gov/Exe/ZyPDF.cgi/2000CAUR.PDF?Dockey=2000CAUR.PDF). That is six months before the J Chem Inf Comput Sci paper (issued 1988-02-01). The basis stays publication.) Verified 5 Oct 2026: dateSource changed (The 1987 EPA report is the earliest formal description (see datePublished).) Proposed 9 Oct 2026: A formal line notation that writes chemical structures as strings for machine exchange and in silico tools, rather than a resolvable identifier.",
   "fsrCategory": "https://w3id.org/fair/fip/terms/FAIR-Enabling-Resource"
  },
  {
